# Interactive workflow: data + renderer for the Workflow tab.
import json

PANELS = [
 {"id":"t1","title":"1  Classify the object","sub":"Neurodata Governance Decision Tool","rec":"Schedule B",
  "out":{"id":"t1out","label":"Tier (highest governs)","lines":["Tier 5  model-associated","Tier 4  revealing derivative","Tier 3  directly identifiable","Tier 2  participant-linkable","Tier 1  aggregate","Provisional until confirmed"]}},
 {"id":"t2","title":"2  Assess the pathway","sub":"Neurodata Transfer and Access Decision Tool","rec":"Schedule C",
  "out":{"id":"t2out","label":"Outcome","lines":["1  Proceed","2  Proceed after safeguards","3  Redesign the pathway","4  Do not proceed"]}},
 {"id":"t3","title":"3  Reassess the transformation","sub":"Derivative and Embedding Governance Decision Tool","rec":"Schedule D",
  "out":{"id":"t3out","label":"Outcome","lines":["1  Retain tier","2  Refer Tier 4","3  Confirm Tier 5","4  Specialist review","5  Do not proceed"]}},
 {"id":"t4","title":"4  Select contract modules","sub":"MDTAA Clause Selection Tool","rec":"Schedules A–D · module list",
  "out":{"id":"t4out","label":"Implementation","lines":["Compile and check consistency","Legal, ethics, repository review","Execute · implement · monitor"]}},
]

# nodes: id, panel, step label, short title, explanation (plain), yes/no effect, record
NODES = [
 # Tool 1
 {"id":"t1s0","p":"t1","step":"Check","title":"Applicability checkpoint","text":"Which statutory access regimes and ex ante data-protection, privacy-impact or privacy-risk assessment requirements may apply, given the data category, holder, purpose, jurisdiction and application date (including the EHDS where relevant)? Legal applicability is assessed separately from the functional-exposure tier.","effect":"Record the potentially applicable regimes and assessment requirements; refer any unresolved applicability question to the responsible legal or institutional reviewer. Completing the checkpoint does not resolve or waive an outstanding legal requirement.","why":"The tier describes functional exposure; it does not tell you which law applies. The checkpoint stops the two being confused."},
 {"id":"t1s1","p":"t1","step":"1","title":"Human neurodata, or derived from it?","text":"MRI, fMRI, EEG, MEG, ECoG, implant and BCI recordings, neural features, embeddings, predictions, or models trained on neurodata.","effect":"No: record 'Not applicable' and follow ordinary data-governance procedures. Yes: continue to Step 2A. Unsure: pause and obtain clarification from the PI, data steward or technical lead.","why":"Scopes the tool to neurodata and its derivatives."},
 {"id":"t1s2a","p":"t1","step":"2A / 2B","title":"Model incorporation and persistence","text":"2A: has the neurodata, or information derived from it, been incorporated into a trained model or persistent computational infrastructure (weights, foundation models, federated systems, synthetic-data generators, gradients, embedding stores)? 2B: is there evidence, or a reasonable possibility, that neural information persists there in a way that supports identification, inference, reconstruction, extraction or profiling?","effect":"2A Yes: Tool 3 becomes mandatory; go to 2B. 2A Unknown: provisional Tier 5 until incorporation is confirmed or excluded (a precaution for legacy datasets with incomplete provenance). 2B Yes or Unknown: provisional Tier 5 pending Tool 3; 2B No: Tool 3 still required because 2A was Yes. Then continue to Step 3.","why":"Model incorporation triggers assessment but does not by itself establish Tier 5; persistence with continuing exposure does."},
 {"id":"t1s4","p":"t1","step":"3 / 4","title":"Derived object with revealing capacity?","text":"Step 3: is the object a derived product (embedding, feature vector, fingerprint, profile, classifier, prediction, reconstruction) rather than original neurodata? Step 4: can that derived object reasonably identify or trace an individual, or reveal, predict, reconstruct or profile neurological, cognitive, emotional or behavioural states of an individual or a sufficiently defined group, given information reasonably available to the intended recipient (or, for public release, the public)?","effect":"Step 4 Yes: Tier 4, controlled access, documented permitted uses and prohibited inferences, specialist review and Tool 3; Tool 2 before any external sharing. Unknown: provisional Tier 4. No, or not derived: continue to Step 5.","why":"Tier 4 is where a transformed object that no longer looks like the source still reveals too much."},
 {"id":"t1s5","p":"t1","step":"5","title":"Direct identifiers or identifying anatomy?","text":"Names, identifying DICOM headers, non-defaced facial anatomy, identifying voice or video, device identifiers and timestamps that enable tracing. A raw EEG, MEG, ECoG or MRI acquisition is not by itself a Yes; linkage solely through a protected code is assessed at Step 6.","effect":"Yes: Tier 3 (secure controlled environment, encryption, role-based access, audit logs, formal access approval; remove unnecessary identifiers; restrict downloads and onward sharing; Tool 2 before transfer or external access). Unknown: provisional Tier 3. No: continue to Step 6.","why":"Direct traceability is the most sensitive conventional category."},
 {"id":"t1s6","p":"t1","step":"6","title":"Participant-level and linkable through a key?","text":"Does the object still contain participant-level neurodata that can be linked back to an individual through a code, repository record or institutional key, wherever that key is held?","effect":"Yes: Tier 2 (controlled-access repository, protected linkage key, encryption, access logs, restrictions on re-identification and onward sharing, a data use or transfer agreement); go to the Final Decision. Unknown: provisional Tier 2. No: go to the Final Decision if a Tier 3, 4 or 5 finding is active, otherwise to Step 7.","why":"Pseudonymised data remain linkable; the custodian of the key is recorded, never the key itself."},
 {"id":"t1s7","p":"t1","step":"7 / 8","title":"Aggregate only, and disclosure risk","text":"Step 7: does the object contain only aggregated or summary-level information with no participant-level record or persistent participant trace? Step 8: could an individual or small group still reasonably be identified through rare conditions, small samples, unique characteristics or accompanying metadata?","effect":"Step 7 No (participant-level, but no identifiers and no key): 'Reassessment required'; revisit Steps 3–6; de-identified data with no reasonably available linkage mechanism are assigned Tier 2A, data with a retained key Tier 2B, identifiable data the applicable higher tier; specialist review only where the evidence is insufficient. Tier 1 is never assigned by default. Step 8 Yes or Unknown: Tier 2 as the minimum, with more aggregation or suppression and reassessment before release. Step 8 No: Tier 1, with the Annexure 1 §7.2 check on whether Tool 3 is needed.","why":"Aggregation is only Tier 1 when the disclosure-risk review supports it."},
 # Tool 2
 {"id":"t2s1","p":"t2","step":"1 / 2","title":"What is planned, and is the tier assigned?","text":"Internal use only, or sharing with another institution, international transfer, repository deposit, cloud-hosted access, AI platform or vendor access, or public release? Has Tool 1 assigned a tier to this object and activity?","effect":"Internal use only: Neuro-TADT not required; apply the Tool 1 safeguards and end. No tier yet: complete Tool 1 first; if Tool 1 cannot establish a tier, Outcome 4.","why":"The pathway assessment needs the tier and its safeguards as its yardstick."},
 {"id":"t2s3","p":"t2","step":"3 / 4","title":"Recipients, or public release?","text":"Named controlled-access recipients (institution, repository, cloud provider, commercial partner, AI developer), recipients not yet identified, or a public or open-access release. Step 4A: can every recipient and sub-processor be identified? Step 4B: can the releasing organisation, repository, hosting provider and sub-processors be identified?","effect":"Named recipients: controlled-access pathway (Steps 4A, 8A, 9A, 10A, 11A, 12A). Public release: public-release pathway (Steps 4B, 8B, 9B, 10B, 11B, 12B), after confirming Tool 1 covers the object under public-access conditions. Unidentified recipients or providers: do not begin; if they cannot be identified, Outcome 4.","why":"Everything downstream depends on who will hold the data and under what access model."},
 {"id":"t2s5","p":"t2","step":"5 / 6","title":"Access mode and jurisdictions","text":"Downloadable copy, remote or controlled access without download, query-only or federated access, or public download, API or repository release. Will the data be transferred to, stored in or remotely accessed from another legal jurisdiction?","effect":"Record what may leave each environment and the tier-appropriate controls on storage, copying, export, logging and output review. Record every originating, receiving, hosting and remotely accessing jurisdiction, including sub-processors; for a public release, future downloader locations are 'unknown or unbounded by design'.","why":"Remote access from abroad is a cross-border pathway even when the data never move."},
 {"id":"t2s7","p":"t2","step":"7 / 7A","title":"Lawful basis, approvals, authorisation","text":"Step 7: have the lawful basis, the transfer or remote-access mechanism, any required DPIA or privacy-risk assessment, and all legal, ethics, institutional and repository approvals been identified and documented, together with the exporter's authority (including upstream conditions)? Step 7A: does the activity require an EHDS permit or other statutory access authorisation?","effect":"Step 7 Yes: record them, mark outstanding approvals as pending; for a public release, record the release authority. No or Unsure: obtain review; if no lawful pathway can be established, Outcome 4. Step 7A then routes to Step 8A (controlled access) or 8B (public release).","why":"Completion of the tool never establishes lawfulness; it records what the law and the approving bodies have decided."},
 {"id":"t2s8","p":"t2","step":"8A / 8B","title":"Can the environment hold the safeguards?","text":"8A: can the receiving or accessing governance environment implement and maintain the safeguards required for the tier (oversight, repository governance, technical controls, contractual compliance, auditability, enforcement, remedies)? 8B: can the tier's safeguards be preserved under the proposed public-release pathway?","effect":"Yes: continue to Step 9A or 9B. No, but another access or release model could preserve them: Outcome 3, redesign. No: Outcome 4. Unsure: obtain evidence; do not begin.","why":"The question is not whether the destination is 'equivalent' but whether the required safeguards can be implemented, verified and maintained there."},
 {"id":"t2s9","p":"t2","step":"9 / 10","title":"Linkage and AI use","text":"9A/9B: will the data be linked with genomic, health-record, wearable, consumer or public-register data, or could a released object be linked with other available datasets in a way that changes its exposure? 10A/10B: will the data or a derivative be used to train, fine-tune, validate, benchmark or deploy a model, or is downstream AI reuse permitted or foreseeable under the release conditions?","effect":"Linkage planned or foreseeable: apply Tool 1 to the linked object before linkage and revise the affected steps. AI use: complete Tool 3 and follow its Final Decision; if Tool 3 requires unresolved specialist review or prohibits the activity, Outcome 4. Public release where AI reuse cannot be controlled: Outcome 3 if the release can be narrowed to controlled access, otherwise Outcome 4.","why":"Linkage and model training are the two ways the functional exposure of a dataset changes after it leaves."},
 {"id":"t2s11","p":"t2","step":"11 / 12 / 13","title":"Purpose, onward sharing, implementation","text":"11A/11B: is the use materially different from the approved purpose, or do the public-use conditions allow reuse beyond it? 12A/12B: may the recipient pass the data on, or does the release mechanism permit copying, mirroring and redistribution? 13: have all safeguards and approvals for the tier and pathway been implemented and documented?","effect":"A different purpose needs its own approvals before it begins; onward sharing and redistribution are either permitted under recorded conditions or prohibited contractually. Step 13: implemented, Outcome 1; implementable, Outcome 2; not implementable in this pathway, Outcome 3 or 4.","why":"Protection has to continue through onward use, not stop at the first recipient."},
 # Tool 3
 {"id":"t3s1","p":"t3","step":"1 / 2 / 3","title":"Trigger, transformation, circulation","text":"Step 1: has or will the neurodata undergo transformation, be used in an AI system, or persist in a model or infrastructure? Routine preprocessing that creates no separately governed derivative, and confirmed Tier 1 aggregate outputs, are exempt (Annexure 1 §7.2); the exemption never covers AI activities flagged by Tool 2. Step 2: what form (features, embeddings, connectivity measures, reconstructed signals, classifiers, synthetic data, trained models, gradients)? Step 3: can the artefact circulate, be queried or deployed without the source data?","effect":"No trigger: record 'Tool 3 not required' and return to the referring tool. Otherwise record the transformation, the artefact and its version, and the controls needed when it is used without the source data.","why":"Transformation is a reassessment point, not the automatic end or continuation of protection."},
 {"id":"t3s4","p":"t3","step":"4","title":"Can the artefact identify or trace a participant?","text":"Directly, through participant linkage, or together with information reasonably available to the intended recipient or, for public release, in the foreseeable public-access environment: reconstructing a participant's neural activity, linking an embedding to a record, or showing whether a participant contributed to the training set.","effect":"Yes: flag for Tool 1 classification and keep identification and linkage safeguards. No: continue; absence of identification does not establish absence of inference or persistence. Unknown: provisional safeguards and technical assessment.","why":"Identification is one route to exposure; the next two steps cover the others."},
 {"id":"t3s5","p":"t3","step":"5 / 6","title":"Inference capability and computational persistence","text":"Step 5: can the artefact reveal, predict, reconstruct or profile neurological, cognitive, emotional or behavioural characteristics of an individual or group, even without identifying anyone? Step 6: has neural information become computationally persistent in a model, embedding, gradient or generator in a way that supports continuing inference, reconstruction, extraction or profiling?","effect":"Step 5 Yes or Unknown: candidate Tier 4, Tier 4 safeguards provisionally. Step 6 Yes: candidate Tier 5, safeguards provisionally; confirmed only through Outcome 3. Step 6 Unknown: provisional Tier 5 and specialist assessment. Model incorporation alone is never sufficient for Tier 5.","why":"Tiers 4 and 5 are defined by what the artefact can do, not by what it is."},
 {"id":"t3s7","p":"t3","step":"7","title":"Leakage testing","text":"Has the artefact or model been evaluated, where technically relevant, for memorisation, training-data extraction, model inversion, membership inference or other leakage, under the intended access conditions?","effect":"Material exposure found: update Steps 4–6 and record a candidate Tier 5 condition where the leakage shows persistent exposure. No material exposure: record scope and limits; not proof of absence beyond the tested conditions. Not tested: obtain an assessment of whether testing is relevant and feasible; if exposure cannot be assessed reliably, Outcome 4.","why":"Testing is the only evidence that can support releasing weights or synthetic data beyond a controlled environment."},
 {"id":"t3s8","p":"t3","step":"8 / 9 / 9A","title":"Provenance and release conditions","text":"Is the transformation history documented (source data, lawful provenance, preprocessing, training, versions, vendors, sub-processors)? Do the proposed access or deployment conditions change exposure (downloads, public release, APIs, fine-tuning, model merging, distillation, commercial deployment)? Have the outputs (features, embeddings, synthetic outputs, weights) been assessed against the applicable authorisation and release conditions?","effect":"Gaps in provenance are carried to the Final Decision and block affected sharing. Conditions that change exposure mark Tool 2 (controlled access or public release) for completion or update. Unassessed outputs are withheld pending assessment and approval.","why":"A model's exposure depends on how it will be hosted and used as much as on what it learned."},
 {"id":"t3s10","p":"t3","step":"10 / 11","title":"Lifecycle controls and manageability","text":"Are retention, withdrawal, deletion, decommissioning and, where feasible, unlearning procedures documented and implementable, including the effects of EHDS opt-out, consent withdrawal and erasure requests? Can the identified exposure be managed through proportionate safeguards throughout the proposed use?","effect":"Yes: proceed to an outcome. Partly: record accepted alternatives (access withdrawal, version retirement, output restrictions, no further training) without treating a proposal as an implemented safeguard. Only after additional safeguards: Outcome with implementation conditions. Unsure: Outcome 4. No: Outcome 5.","why":"A model that cannot be deleted or unlearned needs the alternatives written down before it is shared."},
 # Tool 4
 {"id":"t4s1","p":"t4","step":"1 / 1A","title":"Baseline module, or a public release outside the MDTAA","text":"Is the MDTAA governing the processing, access, transfer or reuse of neurodata or a governed derivative? For a public release governed by repository terms, a public licence or another release instrument rather than an MDTAA with unidentified downstream users, Step 1A records that mechanism and asks whether a separate MDTAA is needed with the repository operator or hosting provider.","effect":"Yes: activate the Baseline Governance Module (Articles 3–7, 9–10, 14–17; Schedules A and B). Public release with no separate MDTAA: record the public-release governance mechanism and end the tool.","why":"Open releases are governed, but not by a bilateral agreement with every downloader."},
 {"id":"t4s2","p":"t4","step":"2 / 3","title":"Cross-border transfer and supplementary safeguards","text":"Activated for any international transfer, storage or remote access (Articles 12, 13, 13A; Annexure 2; Schedule C), recording the jurisdictions and the requirements identified by Tool 2; and, where the Neuro-TADT made supplementary safeguards a condition of proceeding, the Supplementary Safeguards Module specifying each safeguard, the responsible party and the verification evidence.","effect":"Module selection does not establish that the transfer is lawful; it carries the Tool 2 conditions into the contract.","why":"Layer 6 of the legal analysis: contracts are where continuity of protection is made operational."},
 {"id":"t4s4","p":"t4","step":"4–7A","title":"Enhanced protection, derivatives, AI, access","text":"Enhanced Neurodata Protection for Tier 3, 4 or 5 findings, confirmed or provisional; Derivative Neurodata terms for features, embeddings, predictions and synthetic data; AI and Model Governance where models are trained, validated, queried or deployed; Controlled Access, Repository and Infrastructure terms for enclaves, trusted research environments, federated systems and cloud; and the statutory access conditions of an EHDS permit or similar authorisation (Step 7A).","effect":"Each activated module records the obligations found by Tools 1–3, the responsible parties and the review or reassessment requirements; selections are marked provisional where the tier is provisional.","why":"These are the modules a conventional DTA or DUA usually lacks."},
 {"id":"t4s8","p":"t4","step":"8–13","title":"Onward transfer, commercial use, linkage, permitted inference","text":"Whether sub-processors and onward recipients are allowed (Step 8) or prohibited without prior approval (Step 9); commercial use and intellectual property versus research-only use (Steps 10 and 11); which datasets may be linked (Step 12); which inferences are authorised and which prohibited (Step 13).","effect":"Steps 8 and 9, and 10 and 11, must be reconciled so that permitted and prohibited activities are distinct; the linkage module imports the Tool 2 Step 9A/9B assessment.","why":"Technically possible is not the same as authorised; the contract draws that line."},
 {"id":"t4s14","p":"t4","step":"14–20","title":"Audit, deletion, models, community","text":"Enhanced audit and monitoring; return, deletion and certification; retention, version retirement and unlearning for models that outlive the source data; community, collective and Indigenous data governance; benefit sharing and capacity development; project-specific requirements (export controls, device regulation, funder conditions); coordination and precedence with SCCs, DUAs, repository terms and cloud contracts.","effect":"Steps 15 and 16 are reconciled so that deleting the source data and retaining a model are both documented; expiry of another instrument does not by itself end downstream obligations.","why":"Equity and community governance are contractual obligations here, not preamble."},
]
OUTS = {
 "t1out":{"title":"Tier assigned","text":"The highest applicable tier governs, recorded as provisional or confirmed; a tier is confirmed only when all applicable steps and required reviews are complete. Safeguards are cumulative: a Tier 4 or 5 artefact that is still linkable keeps its Tier 2 safeguards.","effect":"Feeds Tool 2; a linked dataset, a changed artefact or a Tool 3 referral returns here for reassessment. If public release is proposed, Tool 2 is entered on the public-release pathway.","why":""},
 "t2out":{"title":"Pathway outcome","text":"1 Proceed subject to applicable approvals; 2 Proceed after specified safeguards are implemented and verified; 3 Redesign the transfer, access or release pathway; 4 Do not proceed and obtain specialist review.","effect":"Outcome 1 requires, for controlled access, identified recipients, an assigned tier, a defined access model and all approvals; for public release, an identified releasing organisation, repository and object version, recorded licence conditions, an assessment under public-access conditions and all release approvals. The outcome is reassessed before any material change.","why":""},
 "t3out":{"title":"Transformation outcome","text":"1 Retain the existing tier; 2 Refer for Tier 4 (return to Tool 1 at Step 3); 3 Confirm Tier 5 (return to Tool 1 at Step 2B); 4 Specialist review required; 5 Do not proceed in the current form.","effect":"A changed tier returns the object to Tool 1; an affected pathway repeats Tool 2; findings feed Tool 4. A previously confirmed Tier 5 is never lowered automatically.","why":""},
 "t4out":{"title":"Implementation","text":"Compile the selected modules, check consistency (especially Steps 8–9, 10–11 and 15–16), obtain legal, ethics, institutional and repository review, execute, implement and monitor.","effect":"A material change in object, tier, purpose, recipient, jurisdiction, infrastructure, access, linkage or AI use reopens the affected tool and the module selection.","why":""},
}
